WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.760
What if you could release a feature to different&nbsp;
groups of users without deployment? Is there a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.920
to effectively test features in production, and&nbsp;
immediately roll them back if needed? Hi, my name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.160
is Dilan Orrino with IBM Cloud. I'll be answering&nbsp;
those questions by discussing feature flags,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.040
or sometimes referred to as feature toggle,&nbsp;
or switches. Feature flags are conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.680
that encompass feature code that allow you to&nbsp;
flip them on and off at will. Okay, let's use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.440
an example. Let's say we've got an ice cream shop&nbsp;
franchise that's looking to expand to a new city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:45.840
and we've got a banner that we want to display&nbsp;
on our website. We'll call this open banner.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:01:00.800
We only want to display this banner to users that&nbsp;
are nearby our new ice cream shop we can do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.680
by using feature flags. There's a&nbsp;
couple benefits to using feature flags.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.600
Number one is we can actually turn&nbsp;
these on or off without deployment.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.680
Number two is we can actually&nbsp;
test directly in production.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.960
And number three we can segment our&nbsp;
users based on different attributes.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:43.760
Okay, there's a couple ways you can do this&nbsp;
one way is by using properties in JSON files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.360
or config maps. There's a better way however&nbsp;
by using a feature flag service. There's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.080
couple benefits to using a feature flag service.&nbsp;
Number one is you can have essentially managed

00:01:59.360 --> 00:01:59.860
place

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.840
for your features, or excuse me your feature&nbsp;
flags. Number two is you can turn these on and off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.160
without modifying

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.680
your properties in your future, in your&nbsp;
apps or web pages. And number three is you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.400
get audit and usage data. It's harder to get&nbsp;
the audit and usage data by using JSON files.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.160
Okay, so now let's go back to our&nbsp;
example we've got our open banner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.640
feature and now let's wrap it&nbsp;
with some feature flag code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.880
And so here's an example, kind of pseudo&nbsp;
code, that you can use if store open

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.840
is enabled.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:03.840
Then we're going to show open banner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.040
so this pseudocode represents our feature code&nbsp;
and the flag that potentially could encompass it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:23.040
Now let's actually put this in production&nbsp;
and make it show showcase to some users. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.120
now that we've got our feature in production&nbsp;
it's not usable to any users right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.880
this is an idea typically displayed with feature&nbsp;
flags called dark launch. Dark launch is when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:40.720
feature is in production but not visible to any&nbsp;
or all users or any or some users, excuse me.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.000
Now we want to introduce the idea of segments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.960
So we've already said that we only want&nbsp;
a certain number of people to view this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.000
people who are nearby our new shop. This will be&nbsp;
our segment A, and a segment is simply users or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.920
groups of users that have attributes tied to them.&nbsp;
So this first one might have current location,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.480
and zip code.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.200
Attributes, this allows users who&nbsp;
are either currently in the location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:22.160
or have already stipulated that they live nearby&nbsp;
to view this feature, but before we do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:29.360
we want to test the feature out on our&nbsp;
own employees. So we would have segment B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.400
of our testers because we want them to be our&nbsp;
employees the attribute might be email ID.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:45.920
Now we can effectively test our feature&nbsp;
in production by flipping this toggle on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.360
So now this feature is on for our testers,&nbsp;
and say maybe something went wrong so we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.120
actually going to flip it off fix it, and&nbsp;
then we'll turn it back on for segment B&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.840
once we're satisfied that everything is&nbsp;
working well. Then we can flip this on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.680
for our segment A now all this is done without&nbsp;
a deployment because our feature is already in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.840
production. All we're doing is making it&nbsp;
visible or not visible to certain users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.960
once it is in production. We can actually&nbsp;
add a little bit of automation to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.400
with our testers, we did it manually, we flipped&nbsp;
it on and off manually, but with feature flags we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.120
can actually add a time element. So let's say we&nbsp;
only want this to be viewable for three weeks, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.400
weeks before grand opening, one week after grand&nbsp;
opening of our new shop. This will be flipped on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.840
and then automatically turned off or not visible&nbsp;
for this segment of users after three weeks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.600
Okay, so now we're getting really good at using&nbsp;
feature flags so our feature flags are potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.880
starting to stack up we might have a couple apps&nbsp;
and a couple websites with a couple different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:59.680
types of features that are flagged. So we've got&nbsp;
maybe app one here, app two, and say a web page.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.920
With a feature flagging service we can actually&nbsp;
group these in collections so that we're a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.320
bit more organized with which feature flags are&nbsp;
tied to, which apps are web pages. So now today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.240
we've learned about returning feature flags on&nbsp;
and off without deployment testing directly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.880
production, and then segmenting those features&nbsp;
based on the user attributes. Thank you for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.640
watching. If you have questions, please drop us&nbsp;
